\begin{abstract}
Tensor-network methods have de-quantized a growing list of quantum algorithms: whenever a quantum state admits a matrix-product (tensor-train) representation of small bond dimension $D$ on $n$ sites, its quantum Fourier transform can be executed classically in time $O(nD^2)$. We assess this proposal against the state at the heart of Shor's factoring algorithm and obtain a decisive negative result with exact, not asymptotic, constants. Let $a$ have multiplicative order $r$ modulo $N$ and let the exponent register have size $Q = rm$. We prove: (i) the full register state $Q^{-1/2}\sum_x |x\rangle|a^x \bmod N\rangle$ has Schmidt rank exactly $r$ across the register cut, with a perfectly flat Schmidt spectrum, entanglement entropy $\log r$ and mutual information $2\log r$; (ii) the periodic comb $c_x = [\,x \equiv x_0 \pmod r\,]$, the actual input of the Fourier transform, has Schmidt rank exactly $\min(C, r/\gcd(r,B))$ across the cut $x = b+Bc$ with $BC=Q$ and $r \le B$, sharpening the folklore estimate $\Theta(\min(r,Q/r))$ to an exact arithmetic law; (iii) the Fourier output is the dual comb of period $m$ conjugated by diagonal phase matrices, hence of rank exactly $\min(C, m/\gcd(m,B))$, so both endpoints are exponentially entangled; (iv) truncation to bond dimension $D$ retains squared overlap at most $D/r$, attained by truncation, with squared Frobenius error at least $2-2\sqrt{D/r}$ (correcting the informally quoted $(D/r)^2$); (v) every classical sampler supported on a set $S$ is at total-variation distance at least $1-|S|/r$ from the ideal output; and (vi) a bond-dimension-$\chi$ representation certifies $a^k=1$ for some $k \le \chi$, so a compressible instance is a classically easy instance, while one ideal sample yields the order and hence a nontrivial factor of $N$. We further refute the natural conjecture that the two Fourier endpoints are complementary ($Q=36$, $r=m=6$, $B=C=6$ makes both endpoints product states) and replace it by an lcm law with simultaneous collapse only when $\lcm(r,m)\mid B$, impossible for a power-of-two block size and an odd order.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The de-quantization programme}

A quantum state on $n$ qubits is a unit vector in $\mathbb{C}^{2^n}$, and writing it down explicitly is out of the question for interesting $n$. The insight underlying tensor-network simulation is that physically relevant states are usually far from generic: they live on a low-dimensional manifold parameterized by a chain of small tensors. A \emph{matrix-product state} (MPS), or \emph{tensor train}, represents the amplitude of $|x_1\cdots x_n\rangle$ as a product $A^{(1)}_{x_1}\cdots A^{(n)}_{x_n}$ of matrices of size at most $D\times D$. The parameter $D$ is the \emph{bond dimension}, and the representation costs $O(nD^2)$ numbers instead of $2^n$.

The bond dimension has an exact operational meaning. For each bipartition of the sites, the state has a coefficient matrix $M$, and its \emph{Schmidt rank}, the ordinary matrix rank of $M$, lower-bounds the bond dimension of any MPS representation across that cut. Once a state has small bond dimension, most of quantum computation becomes classically tractable on it: in particular a state of bond dimension $D$ on $n$ sites can be quantum-Fourier-transformed classically in time $O(nD^2)$, by contracting the transform's two-site gates into the train and recompressing. This is the engine behind a series of successful de-quantizations, and it raises the obvious question about the most famous quantum algorithm of all.

\subsection{The question and the answers}

Shor's algorithm factors $N$ by finding the multiplicative order of a random $a$ coprime to $N$. Its exponential advantage rests entirely on the Fourier step. The question splits in three: what is the entanglement of the state Shor prepares; what is the entanglement of the Fourier transform's input and output across the cuts a tensor train uses; and how badly does truncation fail. This paper answers all three exactly: rank exactly $r$ with a flat spectrum; rank exactly $\min(C, r/\gcd(r,B))$ at the input and $\min(C,m/\gcd(m,B))$ at the output; and truncation fidelity exactly $D/r$, with the low-rank regime coinciding precisely with small order, i.e.\ with classically easy instances.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item An exact Schmidt calculus for \emph{fibre-matching} states (Section~\ref{sec:fibre}), covering every state of the form ``run a classical function in superposition and compare labels''.
  \item Exact entanglement of the Shor register state (Section~\ref{sec:shor}): rank $r$, flat spectrum, entropy $\log r$, mutual information $2\log r$.
  \item A sharp bond-dimension law for the periodic comb (Section~\ref{sec:comb}), strictly sharper than the folklore $\Theta(\min(r,Q/r))$, with an exact characterization of the product-state cuts.
  \item The Fourier output (Section~\ref{sec:output}): the transformed comb is the dual comb dressed by diagonal phases, its distribution is uniform on the $r$ multiples of $m$, and its rank obeys the same law with $r \leftrightarrow m$.
  \item Truncation and sampling bounds (Section~\ref{sec:trunc}): flat-spectrum Eckart--Young with attainment, its Frobenius form, and the total-variation bound $1-|S|/r$.
  \item The endpoint complementarity question settled (Section~\ref{sec:compl}): the naive conjecture refuted by explicit counterexample, and the correct lcm law proved.
  \item The equivalence (Section~\ref{sec:equiv}): small bond dimension certifies small order; one ideal sample factors $N$.
\end{itemize}

\section{Setting and notation}

Throughout, $N>1$ is the integer to be factored and $a$ is a unit modulo $N$ of multiplicative order
\[ r = \ord_N(a) = \min\{k\ge 1 : a^k \equiv 1 \pmod N\}. \]
The exponent register has size $Q$. To state exact results we use the \emph{commensurate model} $Q = rm$ with $m\ge 1$ an integer; this is the standard idealization in which the Fourier peaks are exact, and it is the regime most favourable to a classical emulator. We write $[P]$ for the indicator of a proposition $P$.

A pure state on a bipartite system with index sets $\mathcal A,\mathcal B$ is a matrix $M\in\mathbb{C}^{\mathcal A\times\mathcal B}$; it is \emph{normalized} when $\|M\|_F^2=\sum_{f,g}|M_{f,g}|^2=1$. Its \emph{Schmidt rank} is $\rank M$, its \emph{Schmidt spectrum} the multiset of singular values $w_1\ge w_2\ge\cdots\ge 0$, its \emph{entanglement entropy} $S(M)=-\sum_j w_j^2\log w_j^2$, and (for a pure state) its \emph{mutual information} $I(M)=2S(M)$. The spectrum is \emph{flat} when all nonzero $w_j$ are equal, which for a normalized state of rank $\rho$ means $w_j=\rho^{-1/2}$ and $S(M)=\log\rho$, the maximum compatible with rank $\rho$. Logarithms are natural.

We call $(L,w,R)$ a \emph{Schmidt form} of $M$ when $M = L\,\mathrm{diag}(w)\,R^{*}$ with $L^{*}L=I$, $R^{*}R=I$ and $w\ge 0$; if all entries of $w$ are strictly positive, then $\rank M$ is the length of $w$ and $w$ is the Schmidt spectrum. We write $\BD(M)\le\chi$ when $M=XY$ with $X\in\mathbb{C}^{\mathcal A\times\chi}$, $Y\in\mathbb{C}^{\chi\times\mathcal B}$; this is precisely the existence of a matrix-product representation of bond dimension $\chi$ across the cut, and it implies $\rank M\le\chi$.

A tensor train over the exponent register with $Q = BC$ splits $x$ into a low and a high part,
\[ x = b + Bc, \qquad 0\le b<B,\quad 0\le c<C, \]
and every result below holds for an arbitrary such cut, in particular for qubit cuts where $B$ and $C$ are powers of two.

\section{Fibre-matching states: an exact Schmidt calculus}\label{sec:fibre}

\begin{definition}[Fibre-matching state]
Let $\mathcal A,\mathcal B$ be finite index sets, $\Sigma$ a finite label set, and $u:\mathcal A\to\Sigma$, $v:\mathcal B\to\Sigma$ label maps. For $c\in\mathbb{R}$ the \emph{fibre-matching state} is
\[ M^{u,v,c}_{f,g} = c\,[\,u(f)=v(g)\,]. \]
Write $\phi_u(s)=|u^{-1}(s)|$ for the \emph{fibre cardinality} and $\Match(u,v)=\im(u)\cap\im(v)$ for the set of labels realized on both sides.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact Schmidt decomposition]\label{thm:schmidt}
Define, for $s\in\Match(u,v)$,
\[ L_{f,s}=\frac{[\,u(f)=s\,]}{\sqrt{\phi_u(s)}},\qquad R_{g,s}=\frac{[\,v(g)=s\,]}{\sqrt{\phi_v(s)}},\qquad w_s=c\sqrt{\phi_u(s)\phi_v(s)}. \]
Then $L^{*}L=I$, $R^{*}R=I$ and $M^{u,v,c}=L\,\mathrm{diag}(w)\,R^{*}$. Consequently, for $c\ne 0$,
\[ \rank M^{u,v,c} = |\Match(u,v)|. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Distinct labels have disjoint fibres, so the columns of $L$ (and of $R$) are orthogonal, and they are normalized by construction; the fibre cardinalities are positive exactly on $\Match(u,v)$. Expanding $L\,\mathrm{diag}(w)\,R^{*}$ at $(f,g)$, at most one label $s$ contributes, and it contributes $c$ precisely when $u(f)=v(g)$; the common label then lies in $\Match(u,v)$. All $w_s$ are nonzero for $c\ne 0$, so the number of columns is the rank.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Entanglement in closed form]\label{cor:entropy}
With $p_s=c^2\phi_u(s)\phi_v(s)$ (so that $\sum_{s\in\Match}p_s=1$ exactly when the state is normalized),
\[ S(M^{u,v,c})=\sum_{s\in\Match(u,v)} -p_s\log p_s,\qquad I(M^{u,v,c})=2S(M^{u,v,c}). \]
If the state is \emph{balanced}, i.e.\ $p_s = |\Match(u,v)|^{-1}$ for all matching $s$ --- in particular whenever $u$ and $v$ are injective, or all fibres on each side have equal size --- then the spectrum is flat and $S=\log|\Match(u,v)|$, $I=2\log|\Match(u,v)|$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Tensor-network obstruction]\label{cor:bd}
For $c\ne 0$, every matrix-product representation of $M^{u,v,c}$ across the cut has bond dimension at least $|\Match(u,v)|$; no representation of bond dimension $\chi<|\Match(u,v)|$ exists.
\end{corollary}

Since $\Match(u,v)\subseteq\im(u)\cap\im(v)$, the rank never exceeds $|\Sigma|$, nor $|\mathcal A|$, nor $|\mathcal B|$. The point of Theorem~\ref{thm:schmidt} is that it converts every entanglement question about such states into a counting question about labels, and the counting questions arising from Shor's algorithm are elementary number theory.

\section{The Shor register state}\label{sec:shor}

\begin{definition}
A function $F$ on $\{0,\dots,Q-1\}$ has \emph{exact period $r$} if $F(x)=F(x')\iff x\equiv x'\pmod r$. The \emph{Shor register state} is
\[ \Psi_F=\frac{1}{\sqrt Q}\sum_{x<Q}|x\rangle\otimes|F(x)\rangle,\qquad (\Psi_F)_{x,y}=\frac{[\,F(x)=y\,]}{\sqrt Q}. \]
\end{definition}

The map $x\mapsto a^x\bmod N$ has exact period $r=\ord_N(a)$, since $a^i=a^j$ if and only if $i\equiv j\pmod r$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact entanglement of Shor's state]\label{thm:full}
Let $Q=rm$ with $r,m\ge 1$ and let $F$ have exact period $r$. Then across the exponent/function cut:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=2em]
  \item $\Psi_F$ is normalized;
  \item $\rank\Psi_F=r$ exactly;
  \item the Schmidt spectrum is flat, all $r$ coefficients equal to $r^{-1/2}$;
  \item $S(\Psi_F)=\log r$ and $I(\Psi_F)=2\log r$;
  \item every matrix-product representation across this cut has bond dimension at least $r$, and none of bond dimension $\chi<r$ exists.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Psi_F$ is the fibre-matching state with $u=F$, $v=\mathrm{id}$, $c=Q^{-1/2}$. The identity has all fibres of size $1$ and full image, so $\Match(F,\mathrm{id})=\im(F)$; exact periodicity gives $|\im(F)|=r$ and $\phi_F(y)=m$ for every realized value, because each residue class in $\{0,\dots,rm-1\}$ has exactly $m$ elements. Theorem~\ref{thm:schmidt} then yields rank $r$ and coefficients $w_y=Q^{-1/2}\sqrt m=r^{-1/2}$, all equal; Corollary~\ref{cor:entropy} gives the entropy, and Corollary~\ref{cor:bd} the last item.
\end{proof}

For factoring-relevant instances $r$ is typically comparable to $N$, hence exponential in the bit length. Note that $S=\log(\rank)$ is the equality case of $S\le\log(\rank)$, which holds only for a flat spectrum; computing the entropy and comparing it to the log of the rank is therefore a convenient route to flatness.

\section{The periodic comb: exact bond dimension of the Fourier input}\label{sec:comb}

\begin{definition}[Periodic comb across a cut]
For $Q=BC$, $r\ge 1$, offset $x_0$ and amplitude $\alpha\ne 0$,
\[ \Comb_{B,C,r,x_0}(b,c)=\alpha\,\big[\,(b+Bc)\equiv x_0 \pmod r\,\big],\qquad 0\le b<B,\ 0\le c<C. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[The comb is fibre-matching]\label{lem:comb}
$\Comb_{B,C,r,x_0}$ is the fibre-matching state with label set $\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z}$ and labels
\[ u(b)=b \bmod r,\qquad v(c)=x_0-Bc \bmod r. \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$b+Bc\equiv x_0$ holds iff $b\equiv x_0-Bc$ in $\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Generic upper bound]
$\rank\Comb_{B,C,r,x_0}\le\min\{r,B,C\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}[Cut period]
$\per(r,B)=r/\gcd(r,B)$; equivalently, the least $k\ge 1$ with $r\mid Bk$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp bond dimension of the comb]\label{thm:sharp}
Assume $r\ge 1$, $\alpha\ne 0$ and $r\le B$. Then
\[ \rank\Comb_{B,C,r,x_0}=\min\Big(C,\ \frac{r}{\gcd(r,B)}\Big). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $r\le B$, the low-half label $b\mapsto b\bmod r$ is surjective onto $\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z}$, so $\Match(u,v)=\im(v)$ and the rank is the number of distinct values of $v(c)=x_0-Bc$ for $c<C$. Two indices give the same label iff $r\mid B(c-c')$ iff $\per(r,B)\mid c-c'$; hence $v$ is periodic with period $\per(r,B)$ and injective within a period, giving exactly $\min(C,\per(r,B))$ values.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Coprime cut: full rank]\label{cor:coprime}
If $r\le B$, $r\le C$ and $\gcd(B,r)=1$, then $\rank\Comb=r$ exactly; every matrix-product representation across the cut has bond dimension at least $r$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Aligned cut: product state]\label{cor:aligned}
If $r\le B$, $C\ge 1$ and $r\mid B$, then $\rank\Comb=1$. Conversely, for $C\ge 2$ the rank is $1$ only if $\per(r,B)=1$, i.e.\ only if $r\mid B$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Qubit cuts never compress an odd-order comb]\label{cor:odd}
Let $r>1$ be odd, $B=2^k\ge r$ and $C\ge r$. Then $\gcd(r,B)=1$ and $\rank\Comb=r\ge 2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $r\mid 2^k$ with $r$ odd, then $r$ is coprime to $2^k$ and divides it, so $r\mid 1$, contradicting $r>1$; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:coprime}.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:aligned} is the crux of the assessment: the only cuts that compress the comb are those whose block size is a multiple of the period, and choosing such a block size presupposes knowledge of $r$, the output of the computation. Corollary~\ref{cor:odd} removes even the accidental possibility in the architecture actually built, where registers split at powers of two.

\begin{theorem}[Flat spectrum, no tail]\label{thm:flatcomb}
Suppose $B\le r$, $C\le r$ and $\gcd(B,r)=1$, and normalize the comb. Then both label maps are injective, every matching label carries exactly one pair $(b,c)$, the Schmidt spectrum is flat, and the entanglement entropy equals $\log\rho$ with $\rho=\rank$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$b\mapsto b\bmod r$ is injective for $b<B\le r$. For the right map, $x_0-Bc\equiv x_0-Bc'$ forces $r\mid B(c-c')$, hence $r\mid c-c'$ by coprimality, hence $c=c'$ since $|c-c'|<C\le r$. Injective label maps give all fibre cardinalities equal to $1$; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:entropy}.
\end{proof}

Truncated-MPS emulation on such a state is therefore not approximate but arbitrary: truncation algorithms discard the smallest singular values, and here all singular values are equal.

\section{The other endpoint: the Fourier transform of a comb}\label{sec:output}

Let $\omega_Q=e^{2\pi i/Q}$ and let $Q=rm$. The unnormalized transform of the comb of period $r$ and offset $x_0$ is $\widehat c(y)=\sum_{t<m}\omega_Q^{(x_0+rt)y}$.

\begin{theorem}[Fourier transform of a comb]\label{thm:dft}
For $r,m\ge 1$ and $Q=rm$,
\[ \widehat c(y)=\begin{cases} m\,\omega_Q^{x_0y}, & m\mid y,\\ 0,&\text{otherwise,}\end{cases}\qquad |\widehat c(y)|=m\,[\,m\mid y\,], \]
and the normalized output distribution is
\[ P(y)=\frac{|\widehat c(y)|^2}{rm^2}=\begin{cases}1/r,& m\mid y,\\ 0,&\text{otherwise,}\end{cases}\qquad \sum_{y<Q}P(y)=1. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Factor $\omega_Q^{(x_0+rt)y}=\omega_Q^{x_0y}(\omega_Q^{r})^{ty}$ and note $\omega_Q^{r}=\omega_m$ is a primitive $m$-th root of unity, so $\sum_{t<m}(\omega_m^{y})^{t}$ equals $m$ when $m\mid y$ and $0$ otherwise. There are exactly $r$ multiples of $m$ below $Q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Output state across a cut]
For $Q=BC$ and a phase parameter $j$,
\[ \Out_{B,C,m,j}(b,c)=\alpha\,[\,m\mid(b+Bc)\,]\,\omega_Q^{\,j(b+Bc)}. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Output rank obeys the same law]\label{thm:out}
$\Out_{B,C,m,j}=\mathrm{diag}(\omega_Q^{jb})_{b<B}\cdot\Comb_{B,C,m,0}\cdot\mathrm{diag}(\omega_Q^{jBc})_{c<C}$. Since the diagonal factors are invertible, for $m\le B$
\[ \rank\Out_{B,C,m,j}=\min\Big(C,\ \frac{m}{\gcd(m,B)}\Big); \]
in particular the rank is at least $2$ whenever $C\ge 2$ and $m\nmid B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The phase factorizes as $\omega_Q^{jb}\cdot\omega_Q^{jBc}$ and the support condition is the comb of period $m$ and offset $0$; multiplication by invertible matrices preserves rank, so Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} applies with $r$ replaced by $m$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:out} corrects the widely repeated intuition that the Fourier output of Shor's algorithm is ``nearly a single basis state'' and therefore cheap. It is a comb of $r$ equal peaks, exponentially entangled across generic cuts; the single basis state appears only after measurement.

\section{How badly does low-rank emulation fail?}\label{sec:trunc}

\subsection{Fidelity of truncation: flat-spectrum Eckart--Young}

\begin{theorem}[Flat-spectrum fidelity bound]\label{thm:fid}
Let $M=L\,\mathrm{diag}(w)\,R^{*}$ be in Schmidt form on $\rho$ directions with $|w_j|\le\rho^{-1/2}$ for all $j$ (in particular, $M$ normalized and flat of rank $\rho$). Let $A=P\,\mathrm{diag}(s)\,Q^{*}$ be \emph{any} state in Schmidt form on $D$ directions with $\sum_k s_k^2\le 1$. Then, with $\langle M,A\rangle=\tr(M^{*}A)$,
\[ |\langle M,A\rangle|\le\sqrt{D/\rho},\qquad\text{hence}\qquad |\langle M,A\rangle|^2\le D/\rho. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expanding in the two Schmidt forms, $\langle M,A\rangle=\sum_{j,k}w_js_k\overline{\langle L_j,P_k\rangle}\langle R_j,Q_k\rangle$, so
\[ |\langle M,A\rangle|\le\max_j|w_j|\sum_k s_k\sum_j|\langle L_j,P_k\rangle||\langle R_j,Q_k\rangle|. \]
For fixed $k$, Cauchy--Schwarz and the isometry relations bound the inner sum by $1$. Hence $|\langle M,A\rangle|\le\rho^{-1/2}\sum_k s_k\le\rho^{-1/2}\sqrt D$ by Cauchy--Schwarz on $D$ terms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The bound is attained]\label{thm:attain}
Let $M$ be normalized and flat of rank $\rho$, and let $A$ be obtained by keeping any $D\ge 1$ of its Schmidt directions and renormalizing. Then $|\langle M,A\rangle|^2=D/\rho$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Frobenius form]
With $M,A$ normalized as in Theorem~\ref{thm:fid}, $\ \|M-A\|_F^2\ge 2-2\sqrt{D/\rho}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Truncated emulation of Shor's state]
Let $Q=rm$ and let $F$ have exact period $r$. Every normalized state $A$ of Schmidt rank at most $D$ satisfies $|\langle\Psi_F,A\rangle|^2\le D/r$. With $r$ exponential in the input size and $D$ polynomial, the fidelity of any tensor-train emulation is exponentially small.
\end{corollary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:fid} does not require the approximant to be a truncation of $M$: it bounds every low-rank state, however constructed, including the output of variational optimization. It also corrects a value quoted informally in the de-quantization literature: the fidelity of a rank-$D$ truncation of a flat rank-$r$ state is $D/r$, not $(D/r)^2$. The corrected value is larger --- truncation is less catastrophic than folklore by a square root --- and remains exponentially small. The same proof gives a version with no flatness assumed: if all Schmidt coefficients of $M$ have modulus at most $W$, then $|\langle M,A\rangle|^2\le DW^2$.

\subsection{Sampling: small support is fatal}

\begin{theorem}[Support bound in total variation]\label{thm:tv}
Let $P$ be the ideal Fourier output on $\{0,\dots,rm-1\}$, i.e.\ $P(y)=1/r$ for $m\mid y$ and $0$ otherwise, and let $\mathcal Q$ be any probability distribution supported on a set $S$. Then
\[ \dTV(P,\mathcal Q)\ \ge\ 1-\frac{|S|}{r}; \]
in particular $\dTV(P,\mathcal Q)\ge 1/2$ whenever $2|S|\le r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Total variation dominates the excess of $P$ over $\mathcal Q$ on any event; take the event $\mathcal P\setminus S$, where $\mathcal P$ is the set of $r$ peaks. Then $\mathcal Q$ gives it zero mass while $P$ gives it at least $1-|S|/r$.
\end{proof}

\section{Complementarity of the two endpoints: refuted, then repaired}\label{sec:compl}

\begin{theorem}[Refutation]\label{thm:refute}
For $Q=36$, $r=m=6$ and the balanced cut $B=C=6$, both endpoints are product states:
\[ \rank\Comb_{6,6,6,x_0}=1\quad\text{and}\quad\rank\Out_{6,6,6,j}=1. \]
More generally, whenever $r\mid B$ and $m\mid B$, both endpoint ranks equal $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[Cut period as an order]\label{lem:order}
For $r\ge 1$ and all $B,k$: $\ r\mid Bk\iff\per(r,B)\mid k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $g=\gcd(r,B)$, $r=g\per(r,B)$, $B=gB'$ with $\gcd(\per(r,B),B')=1$; then $r\mid Bk\iff\per(r,B)\mid B'k\iff\per(r,B)\mid k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[lcm law for cut periods]\label{thm:lcm}
For all $r,m\ge 1$ and every $B$,
\[ \per(\lcm(r,m),B)=\lcm\big(\per(r,B),\per(m,B)\big). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:order}, the left side is the least $k$ with $r\mid Bk$ and $m\mid Bk$, i.e.\ with $\per(r,B)\mid k$ and $\per(m,B)\mid k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Corrected complementarity]\label{thm:corrcompl}
Assume $\alpha\ne 0$, $C\ge 1$, $r\le B$ and $m\le B$. Then
\[ \min\big(C,\ \per(\lcm(r,m),B)\big)\ \le\ \rank\Comb_{B,C,r,x_0}\cdot\rank\Out_{B,C,m,j}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The two ranks are $\min(C,\per(r,B))$ and $\min(C,\per(m,B))$ by Theorems~\ref{thm:sharp} and~\ref{thm:out}. By Theorem~\ref{thm:lcm} the left side is $\min(C,\lcm(\per(r,B),\per(m,B)))$, and $\lcm(x,y)\le xy$ together with $\min(C,xy)\le\min(C,x)\min(C,y)$ finishes the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Simultaneous collapse requires alignment]
If $C\ge 2$, $r\le B$, $m\le B$ and both endpoint ranks equal $1$, then $\lcm(r,m)\mid B$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Qubit cuts of odd-order combs are never aligned]
If $r>1$ is odd then $r\nmid 2^k$ for every $k$; with $B=2^k\ge r$ and $C\ge r$ the input rank is the full $\min(C,r)\ge 2$, and no simultaneous collapse occurs.
\end{corollary}

The emulator's escape hatch therefore exists --- the naive conjecture was too strong --- but it is exactly the aligned cut $\lcm(r,m)\mid B$, which requires knowing $r$ and, for the odd orders that arise constantly in practice, is unavailable at any power-of-two block size.

\section{The decisive equivalence}\label{sec:equiv}

\begin{theorem}[Small bond dimension certifies a small order]\label{thm:cert}
Let $a$ have order $r$, let $Q=rm$, and suppose the Shor register state admits a matrix-product representation of bond dimension $\chi$ across the register cut. Then there exists $k$ with $1\le k\le\chi$ and $a^k=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:full} the Schmidt rank is $r$, and bond dimension bounds rank from below, so $r\le\chi$; take $k=r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If the order exceeds $\chi$, no bond-dimension-$\chi$ representation exists. Contrapositively, a polynomial-size tensor train exists only when the order is polynomially small --- and a polynomially small order is found classically in polynomial time by computing $a,a^2,a^3,\dots$ until $1$ recurs.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Peak samples determine the order]\label{thm:peak}
Let $Q=rm$ and let $y=km$ be a peak of the ideal output. Then
\[ \frac{Q}{\gcd(y,Q)}=\frac{r}{\gcd(k,r)}, \]
which divides $r$ and equals $r$ exactly when $\gcd(k,r)=1$. The reduced denominator is computable from $y$ and $Q$ alone, in practice by continued fractions.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\gcd(km,rm)=m\gcd(k,r)$, so $Q/\gcd(y,Q)=rm/(m\gcd(k,r))=r/\gcd(k,r)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Order to factor]\label{thm:factor}
Let $N>1$ and let $x$ be an integer with $N\mid x^2-1$, $N\nmid x-1$ and $N\nmid x+1$. Then $d=\gcd(x-1,N)$ satisfies $d\mid N$ and $1<d<N$. Consequently, if $a$ modulo $N$ has even order $r$ and $a^{r/2}\not\equiv-1$, then $N$ has a nontrivial factor computable by one gcd.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $d=1$ then $N\mid x+1$ from $N\mid(x-1)(x+1)$, a contradiction; if $d=N$ then $N\mid x-1$, also a contradiction. For the application take $x=a^{r/2}$: then $x^2\equiv 1$, $x\not\equiv1$ since $r/2<r$ is below the order, and $x\not\equiv-1$ by hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[De-quantizing Shor equals classical factoring]
A classical algorithm that, given $(a,N)$ and $Q$, samples in polynomial time from the ideal Fourier output distribution yields a polynomial-time classical factoring algorithm: sample, extract the order by Theorem~\ref{thm:peak}, and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:factor}, repeating over random bases.
\end{corollary}

Together with Theorem~\ref{thm:cert} this closes the loop. Suppose a polynomial-time tensor-network emulation of Shor's Fourier step is exhibited for factoring-relevant instances. If it genuinely represents the states, its bond dimension lower-bounds nothing less than the order, so the instance was classically easy; if it only reproduces the samples, it \emph{is} a polynomial-time classical factoring algorithm. Either way no new quantum-to-classical reduction has been achieved.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A --- Exact Schmidt data of a fibre-matching state.} Tabulate the fibre cardinalities $\phi_u,\phi_v$ in one pass each, form $\Match=\im u\cap\im v$, and output rank $|\Match|$, coefficients $w_s=c\sqrt{\phi_u(s)\phi_v(s)}$ and entropy $-\sum p_s\log p_s$ with $p_s=w_s^2$. Cost $O(|\mathcal A|+|\mathcal B|+|\Sigma|)$: no singular value decomposition.

\paragraph{Algorithm B --- Bond dimension of a comb across a cut.} Given $r\le B$ and $C$, return $\min(C,r/\gcd(r,B))$ by one Euclidean gcd, cost $O(\log r)$, in place of an $O(BC\min(B,C))$ SVD.

\paragraph{Algorithm C --- Truncation diagnostics.} Given a flat rank $\rho$ and a target bond dimension $D$, return fidelity $D/\rho$, squared Frobenius error $2-2\sqrt{D/\rho}$, and the sampler bound $1-D/\rho$. Cost $O(1)$.

\paragraph{Algorithm D --- Sample to factor.} From a sample $y$: take the largest continued-fraction denominator of $y/Q$ not exceeding $N$, call it $\tilde r$; verify $a^{\tilde r}\equiv 1$; if $\tilde r$ is even and $x=a^{\tilde r/2}\not\equiv-1$, return $\gcd(x\mp 1,N)$; otherwise resample. Cost $O(\log^3 N)$ per attempt.

\section{Numerical corroboration}

A companion program verifies every quantitative claim on explicit small instances, using exact rational Gaussian elimination for ranks and a Jacobi eigensolver for spectra. For $N=15,a=7$ ($r=4$), $N=21,a=2$ ($r=6$) and $N=35,a=3$ ($r=12$) the measured Schmidt ranks are $4,6,12$, all singular values equal $r^{-1/2}$ to machine precision, and the entropies are $1.386294$, $1.791759$, $2.484907$ nats, matching $\log r$ exactly. Across a battery of cuts the measured comb rank agrees with $\min(C,r/\gcd(r,B))$ in every case, including the collapses at $r\mid B$ and the full ranks $5,7,15,21$ at power-of-two block sizes with odd orders. For $B,C\le r$ with $\gcd(B,r)=1$ the ratio of largest to smallest Schmidt weight is $1.000000$ and the participation ratio equals the rank. The transform of the comb is supported exactly on the multiples of $m$, each of probability $1/r$, and the measured rank of the phase-dressed output agrees with $\min(C,m/\gcd(m,B))$ at every admissible cut. With $r=64$, samplers supported on $|S|$ frequencies realize total-variation distance exactly $1-|S|/r$. For $\rho=1024$ the truncation fidelity is $D/\rho$ to six decimals, confirming $D/\rho$ rather than $(D/\rho)^2$. For $N=15,21,33,35$ the peaks with index coprime to $r$ return the exact order by continued fractions and the gcd step returns $3\cdot5$, $3\cdot7$, $3\cdot11$, $5\cdot7$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What has been ruled out, and what has not}
The tensor-train Fourier theorem is not challenged here; it is a correct statement about states of small bond dimension. What has been established is that its hypothesis fails for Shor's states, exactly and at both endpoints of the transform, and that the failure is not a matter of constants: the required bond dimension is the order itself, and the spectrum is flat, so no approximate version of the hypothesis holds either. Equally, nothing here shows that factoring is hard: it shows that this route is a closed loop, succeeding only where the problem was already easy.

\subsection{Why flatness is the crucial invariant}
Rank alone would not settle the question: a state can have exponential rank and still be well approximated at small bond dimension if its Schmidt spectrum decays, which is the everyday situation in condensed-matter simulation. The relevant property here is that the spectra are maximally undecaying, $S=\log(\rank)$. Flatness is not an accident but a consequence of a group action: the comb is a coset of a subgroup of $\mathbb{Z}/Q\mathbb{Z}$, and the fibres of the Shor state are cosets of $r\mathbb{Z}/Q\mathbb{Z}$; cosets have equal cardinality, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:schmidt} equal fibre cardinalities force equal Schmidt coefficients. Any algorithm whose intermediate state is a uniform superposition over a coset of a hidden subgroup inherits the same rigidity.

\subsection{The knowledge paradox}
The cuts that compress the state are exactly those aligned with the hidden period, and locating such a cut is the computation. Equivalently, the comb's support is recognized by an $r$-state deterministic automaton (track $x\bmod r$), and the bond dimension across a cut is the number of automaton states still distinguishable there --- which is $r$ up to the alignment discount $\gcd(r,B)$. A second route says the same thing: the comb decomposes as $\frac1r\sum_{j<r}\omega^{-jx_0}|\mathrm{wave}_j\rangle$ with each wave a product state, and the transform sends each wave to a basis state, so the output is a sum of $r$ basis states --- a genuine decomposition whose cost is $O(r)$ to write down. The only polynomial-time ``Fourier transform of the comb'' is one handed the pair $(r,x_0)$, i.e.\ handed the answer.

\subsection{Relation to other de-quantizations}
Successful de-quantizations typically exploit either sampling access to a low-rank data matrix or genuinely small entanglement. Shor's algorithm has neither. The diagnostic suggested by this assessment for future claims is: ask for the Schmidt \emph{spectrum}, not just the rank, and ask for it at the actual input of the transform. The uniform superposition preceding modular exponentiation is a product state of rank $1$, but it is not the Fourier input, and quoting it would be a category error.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Incommensurate registers.} Our exact statements assume $Q=rm$; real implementations take $Q$ a power of two, and the output peaks broaden. Ranks are expected only to increase, but an exact lower bound in the incommensurate case would complete the picture.

\textbf{Noisy and approximate combs.} What is the Schmidt spectrum of a comb corrupted by depolarizing noise, and at what noise level does an area-law-like decay appear? Section~\ref{sec:trunc} is the right starting point, since it bounds every low-rank approximant, not merely truncations.

\textbf{Other tensor-network topologies.} Tree tensor networks and PEPS-like layouts admit different cuts; the cut-period law suggests the same knowledge paradox applies, but a complete statement over all balanced trees is open.

\textbf{Hidden subgroup generalization.} An exact Schmidt-rank law for the standard hidden-subgroup state over a general finite abelian group would generalize every result here and cover discrete logarithm and Simon's problem as corollaries.

\textbf{Sharper sampling lower bounds.} Theorem~\ref{thm:tv} is tight for supports but silent about full-support samplers of small entropy; a bound of the form $\dTV\ge 1-e^{H}/r$ in terms of a R\'enyi entropy $H$ would cover a wider class of emulators.

\textbf{A resource-theoretic reading.} The cut period $\per(r,B)=r/\gcd(r,B)$ behaves like a resource monotone for cuts, with Theorem~\ref{thm:lcm} as its composition rule; making this precise may give a clean language for how much of a hidden period a decomposition can see.

\section{Conclusion}

The state at the input of Shor's quantum Fourier transform has Schmidt rank exactly $\min(C,r/\gcd(r,B))$ across the cut $x=b+Bc$; the output has rank exactly $\min(C,m/\gcd(m,B))$ with $m=Q/r$; and the full two-register state has rank exactly $r$ with a flat spectrum, entropy $\log r$ and mutual information $2\log r$. Truncating to bond dimension $D$ leaves fidelity exactly $D/r$ and squared error at least $2-2\sqrt{D/r}$, and a sampler supported on $S$ frequencies is at total-variation distance at least $1-|S|/r$ from the ideal output. Compression across a cut is possible only when the cut is aligned with the period --- only for someone who already knows the answer --- and never at a power-of-two cut of an odd order. Finally, a polynomially small bond dimension certifies a polynomially small order, hence a classically easy instance, while a polynomial-time classical sampler of the ideal output is a polynomial-time classical factoring algorithm. The coherent superposition inside the Fourier transform is irreducible.

